  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily severe weather threat through the end of \nthe week.\n\nAloft, upper level ridging over the Ohio River Valley slowly\nshifts eastward this afternoon as a shortwave trough pivots\nover the forecast area. At the surface, a strong low pressure\nsystem tracks east across Canada through the end of the week\nwith the associated fronts lingering near the forecast area. On\nFriday, the associated cold front tracks across the forecast\narea. Daily shower and thunderstorm chances continue through \nthe end of the week across the forecast area with a hot and\nhumid airmass yielding ample CAPE (1500-2500 J/kg)each\nafternoon.  \n\nFor this afternoon, highest chances remain in NE \nMaryland where the Storm Prediction Center has a slight risk for\nsevere weather. Primary hazard types are damaging wind gusts \nand large hail. The forecast for Wednesday through Friday\nremains uncertain regarding timing and thunderstorm coverage.\nShould thunderstorm development coincide with peak daytime\nheating, instability will yield strong to severe thunderstorms\nacross the forecast area. The best chance for severe weather is\non Thursday as the aforementioned cold front approaches the \nforecast area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions yield possible record high \n minimum temperatures this week\n\nA hot and humid airmass builds overhead this week with heat\nindices soaring into the upper 90s to low 100s each afternoon.\nHeat indices remain below criteria today and Wednesday, maxing\nout at around 100-103 along the metros. Coverage of heat indices\nexceeding criteria remains isolated and brief for tomorrow, so\nrefrained from issuing a Heat Advisory for Wednesday. However,\nThursday has the potential to be the warmest day of the week\nwith heat indices forecast to exceed 100 in the valleys west of\nthe Blue Ridge and 105 along the I-95 corridor. There remains\nsome uncertainty regarding cloud coverage and convection on\nThursday and how that effects the high temperatures. Should\nsevere weather occur during the day, heat indices will struggle\nto reach criteria in the afternoon. However, if severe weather\noccurs later in the afternoon/evening, peak daytime heating will\nhave high temperatures nearing the mid to upper 90s. Humid\nconditions continue overnight, with near record high minimum\ntemperatures possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Decreasing heat & thunderstorm chances leading \ninto Labor Day weekend. \n\nOn Saturday, high pressure builds at the surface over the Great \nLakes further advancing a cold front south across our region. Drier \nair trailing behind this boundary will stabilize conditions and \nreduce thunderstorm chances through the weekend. Brief showers or \nthunderstorms remain possible as a few disturbances round the base \nof the upper-level trough, but overall, no hazardous weather is \nexpected through the beginning of next week. Through the long \nweekend, day time temperatures continue to drop back into seasonable \nconditions for early September. With dew-points in the low to mid \n60s, seasonable temperatures, and relatively low precipitation \nchances, Labor Day weekend is shaping up to be a pleasant September \nweekend.",
